 🗳️ Good Monday morning. It's Election Eve. Smart Brevity™ count: 1,480 words ... 5½ mins. Thanks to Noah Bressner for orchestrating. Edited by Bill Kole. 

 🎤 President Trump will speak at the Lincoln Memorial Reflecting Pool at 2 p.m. ET. We're told he'll give an update on his D.C. restorations and deliver a midterm closing message heralding administration and GOP accomplishments. Go deeper . 

 🏛️ Sen. Chris Murphy (D-Conn.) is "awake and alert" at Johns Hopkins Hospital in Baltimore after a stroke on Saturday. Murphy, 53, experienced numbness in his arm while attending his son's hockey game. Go deeper . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Michigan Senate shocker 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Michigan Republican Senate candidate Mike Rogers and Rep. Haley Stevens (D-Mich.) attend a service at Perfecting Church in Detroit yesterday. Photo: Jose Juarez/AP Two days before midterms, a stunning Democratic defection rocked Michigan's Senate race after Rep. Haley Stevens (D-Mich.) endorsed Republican nominee Mike Rogers — triggering resignations of her top staff and calls for her expulsion from the party.

 Why it matters: Stevens aggravated two Democratic sore spots — divisions over Israel and fears of losing Black voters — in a race that could decide the Senate. 🥊 Between the lines: Nate Silver wrote that if El-Sayed loses, "it will now be much harder to blame his progressivism since people can just blame Haley Stevens instead."

 A N.Y. Times/Siena poll released last night showed El-Sayed leading Rogers by 11 points, 53% to 42%. The endorsement capped a long-running feud between Stevens, a pro-Israel moderate, and Democratic nominee Abdul El-Sayed, a progressive who narrowly defeated her in the primary.

 El-Sayed accused Stevens on Friday of trying to relitigate the primary after she pressed him to more forcefully condemn an outside ad linking U.S. aid to Israel to rising prices. Stevens said the ad suggested that Jews "are responsible for America's economic woes." 🔎 Zoom in: Stevens sat with Rogers yesterday at a Black church in Detroit, where she praised his support for Black-owned businesses.

 Stevens later posted a video on X endorsing Rogers. She said El-Sayed told her she was no longer welcome in his campaign. CNN's Anderson Cooper interviews Rep. Haley Stevens (D-Mich.) last night. Screenshot: CNN The intrigue: Stevens privately assured top Democrats, including Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer and Rep. Jamie Raskin (D-Md.), that she wouldn't endorse Rogers.

 As recently as early yesterday, Stevens told former Michigan Gov. Jim Blanchard she planned to attend church with El-Sayed, according to The Washington Post . "This is not the Haley I know," Blanchard told the Post. "This is not the Haley I had dinner with on Friday night." State of play: Several Stevens staffers resigned shortly after her announcement.

 Her chief of staff, Justin German, said he was "shocked and heartbroken" and that "many members of our team have resigned, including myself." Schumer , who backed Stevens during the primary, called the endorsement a "despicable betrayal." 👂 What we're hearing: House Democrats have privately discussed ousting Stevens from their caucus.

 Stevens, who's leaving office in January, was also removed from several House Democratic text chains. Share this story ... Axios' Andrew Solender, Andrew Pantazi, Alex Isenstadt and Holly Otterbein contributed. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 2. 💸 Costly red-state rescues 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Republicans are burning tens of millions of last-minute ad dollars to try to rescue three red-state senators who they say ignored months of warnings that their campaigns were in danger, Axios' Alex Isenstadt writes. 

 Why it matters: Senate races in Kansas, South Carolina and Mississippi are normally GOP layups. This year, Republicans have diverted resources from swing-state battles to defend them. Frustration with Sens. Roger Marshall (Kan.), Darline Graham (S.C.) and Cindy Hyde-Smith (Miss.) comes amid broader GOP finger-pointing as polls show a nationwide backlash against President Trump.

 A national GOP operative involved in several key Senate races told us: "Democrats may win these seats in a wave ... but not a blue wave. It's the wave of arrogance [and] laziness ... that washed over these GOP incumbents and made them think they were invincible." 🧮 By the numbers: National Republicans have spent $33 million in Kansas, $21 million in South Carolina and $2.4 million in Mississippi, according to data compiled by campaign finance expert Rob Pyers.

 Trump has also spent valuable campaign time in all three states. 👀 Behind the scenes: Republicans say the three senators ignored warnings to raise more money and build stronger campaign operations.

 The most complaints center on Marshall, who has raised just $7.5 million compared with $19 million for his Democratic opponent, Adam Hamilton. Republicans describe Marshall's campaign as overconfident. About a month ago, his team told aides to other GOP senators that the race would be effectively over within 10 days once it released opposition research on Hamilton. It wasn't. Share this story . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 3. 💰 Biggest midterm megadonors 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: The New York Times . Chart: Noah Bressner/Axios The top 20 donors have poured about $2 billion into federal races for this year's midterm elections, The New York Times' Matt Zdun and Teddy Schleifer write . 

 That's 15% of all the money that will be spent on ads this cycle. Elon Musk ($446 million) towers over every other midterm donor, contributing nearly three times as much as the next-biggest donor.

 That figure excludes tens of millions in additional contributions made after Oct. 14 or to groups that don't report to the FEC. 🔬 Zoom in: The tech industry is flexing its political muscle with venture-capital firm Andreessen Horowitz and its founders contributing $116 million, overwhelmingly to Republican causes, including super PACs pushing AI- and crypto-friendly policies.

 OpenAI co-founder Greg Brockman and his wife have also poured money into a pro-AI super PAC, though they've since distanced themselves from the group. On the Democratic side, tech investor Reid Hoffman has spent nearly $17 million backing Texas Senate candidate James Talarico. Top 20 (via X) ... Keep reading (gift link). 

 
 
