 4. 📊 Final polls: Blue Senate surge 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Michigan Democratic Senate candidate Abdul El-Sayed speaks during a campaign rally in Detroit yesterday. Photo: Julia Demaree Nikhinson/AP In the final set of New York Times/Siena polls before tomorrow's election, Democrats are ahead in all seven battleground states likely to decide the Senate.

 Since a September poll, Democrats gained in six of the states (Iowa, Kansas, Maine, Michigan, Ohio and Texas) and lost ground in Alaska. 🧮 By the numbers: Michigan Democratic Senate candidate Abdul El-Sayed leads Republican rival Mike Rogers by 11 points.

 Texas Democrat James Talarico has a 7-point lead over state Attorney General Ken Paxton. Democratic candidates also lead in Maine, Ohio and Kansas, and narrowly in Alaska — all states with incumbent Republican senators. The race in Iowa is effectively tied. Between the lines: In six of the seven states (every one but Maine), the Democratic Senate candidate was viewed more favorably than the Republican.

 To take control of the Senate, Democrats have to hold every seat they have and flip at least four Republican-held seats. If these polls are right, they win. NYT gift link ... More data . 

 
 
